  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="HHD790CCB2EDB33AB49E3957CEAFD4E7D7" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="HHFB845F6A13C048AB10E053139FD9FDD6" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="StyleHeading1Arial" style="margin-left: -.1in; tab-stops: 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 12.0pt; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: -.1in; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;NOTE A &amp;#8211; ORGANIZATION, OPERATIONS AND BASIS OF PRESENTATION:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Organization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;.&amp;#160; Florida Gas Transmission Company, LLC (&amp;#8220;Florida Gas&amp;#8221;) is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Citrus, LLC (&amp;#8220;Citrus&amp;#8221;). &amp;#160;Citrus is owned 50% by El Paso Citrus Holdings, Inc., a wholly-owned indirect subsidiary of Kinder Morgan, Inc., and 50% by CrossCountry Citrus, LLC, a wholly-owned subsidiary of Energy Transfer Interstate Holdings, LLC (&amp;#8220;ETIH&amp;#8221;). &amp;#160;ETIH is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Energy Transfer LP. &amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Operations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;.&amp;#160; Florida Gas is an open-access interstate natural gas pipeline system with a mainline capacity of 4.4 billion cubic feet per day and approximately 5,365 miles of pipeline extending from south Texas through the Gulf Coast region of the United States to south Florida.&amp;#160; Florida Gas is engaged in the interstate transportation of natural gas and as such, Florida Gas is subject to the jurisdiction of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (&amp;#8220;FERC&amp;#8221;).&amp;#160; Florida Gas&amp;#8217; pipeline system receives gas from various onshore and offshore natural gas producing basins.&amp;#160; Florida Gas is the principal transporter of natural gas to the Florida energy market, delivering approximately 60% of the natural gas consumed in the state.&amp;#160; In addition, Florida Gas&amp;#8217; pipeline system operates and maintains interconnects with major interstate and intrastate natural gas pipelines, which provide Florida Gas&amp;#8217; customers access to diverse natural gas producing regions.&amp;#160; Florida Gas&amp;#8217; customers include electric utilities, independent power producers, industrial end-users and local distribution companies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;.&amp;#160; These interim financial statements should be read in conjunction with the financial statements and notes thereto contained in Florida Gas&amp;#8217; FERC Form 2 for the year ended December 31, 2025. &amp;#160;These financial statements are unaudited, but in the opinion of management, all adjustments have been made that are necessary to fairly state the financial position, results of operations and cash flows for the periods presented.&amp;#160; Due to the seasonal nature of Florida Gas&amp;#8217; operations, the results as presented for this interim period are not necessarily indicative of the results to be expected for the full year.&amp;#160; Certain information and notes which would substantially duplicate the disclosures contained in the most recent FERC Form 2 have been omitted.&lt;a name="_MON_1432461091"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a name="_MON_1432637183"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a name="_MON_1416727258"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: -.1in; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: -.1in; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;a name="_Hlk204594534"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;NOTE B &amp;#8211; COMMITMENTS, CONTINGENCIES AND ENVIRONMENTAL LIABILITIES:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Litigation Regarding Jeanerette Lumber &amp;amp; Shingle Co., L.L.C.&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;On September 27, 2018, Jeanerette Lumber &amp;amp; Shingle Co., L.L.C. (&amp;#8220;Plaintiff&amp;#8221;) filed suit against Florida Gas, ExxonMobil Oil Corporation, Chevron U.S.A. Inc., and Chevron U.S.A. Holdings, Inc. (collectively, &amp;#8220;Defendants&amp;#8221;) seeking to hold Defendants liable for compensatory damages and attorneys' fees and costs arising out of the Defendants&amp;#8217; pipeline operations on Plaintiff&amp;#8217;s property located in Louisiana&amp;#8217;s Atchafalaya Basin.&amp;#160; In 1963, Plaintiff&amp;#8217;s predecessor-in-interest granted Florida Gas and others a pipeline right-of-way over land Plaintiff now owns (the &amp;#8220;Right-of-Way Agreement&amp;#8221;).&amp;#160; According to Plaintiff, the Right-of-Way Agreement imposed certain obligations on Florida Gas, such as maintaining the width of the pipeline canal to 40 feet and arranging the spoil along the canal banks, so it does not impede drainage across the property.&amp;#160; Plaintiff contends that the failure to comply with these provisions has affected hydrology and harmed the ecologically valuable cypress-tupelo swampland on Plaintiff&amp;#8217;s and nearby properties.&amp;#160; Plaintiff seeks $18 million in restoration damages for the Florida Gas canal.&amp;#160; Florida Gas contests Plaintiff&amp;#8217;s interpretation of the Right-of-Way Agreement and contends that it has upheld its right of ways in good condition and maintenance in compliance with all state regulatory requirements.&amp;#160; Florida Gas further contends it does not owe any damages to Plaintiff, because Plaintiff has not shown causation between its right of way maintenance and any ecological damage.&amp;#160; The trial took place on June 2, 2025, in St. Martin Parish, Louisiana. &amp;#160;After Plaintiff presented its case to the jury, the judge granted Florida Gas&amp;#8217; Motion for a Directed Verdict, ending the trial because the Plaintiff failed to prove its case.&amp;#160; Plaintiff filed a motion for the trial judge to recuse himself, alleging that the trial judge did not disclose that one of his new law clerks was a distant cousin of a Florida Gas&amp;#8217; trial attorney.&amp;#160; A magistrate judge appointed by the appellate court heard and denied the motion, but Plaintiff filed an appeal up to the Third Circuit Court of Appeals.&amp;#160; The motion is pending oral argument.&amp;#160; Plaintiff also filed a Motion for New Trial, which Florida Gas had opposed as procedurally improper and unnecessary.&amp;#160; On November 7, 2025, the district court held a hearing on the Plaintiff&amp;#8217;s motion for new trial, which the trial court denied.&amp;#160; Plaintiff filed a motion for appeal, and the parties are waiting for the district court to prepare the full record to transmit to the appellate court.&amp;#160; Once the court of appeal receives the complete record, it will issue a briefing schedule.&amp;#160; &amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Liabilities for Litigation and Other Claims.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160; Florida Gas records accrued liabilities for litigation and other claim costs when management believes a loss is probable and reasonably estimable. &amp;#160;When management believes there is at least a reasonable possibility that a material loss or an additional material loss may have been incurred, Florida Gas discloses (i) an estimate of the possible loss or range of loss in excess of the amount accrued; or (ii) a statement that such an estimate cannot be made. &amp;#160;As of March 31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, Florida Gas recorded litigation and other claim-related accrued liabilities of $0.3 million in Other deferred credits, Account 253.&amp;#160; Currently, Florida Gas is not able to estimate the possible losses or range of possible losses in excess of amounts accrued. &amp;#160;Except for the matters discussed above, Florida Gas does not have any material litigation or other claim contingency matters assessed as probable or reasonably possible that would require disclosure in the financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Environmental Matters.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160; Florida Gas&amp;#8217; operations are subject to extensive federal, state and local environmental and safety laws and regulations that require expenditures to ensure compliance, including related to air emissions and wastewater discharges, at operating facilities and for remediation at current and former facilities as well as waste disposal sites.&amp;#160; Historically, Florida Gas&amp;#8217; environmental compliance costs have not had a material adverse effect on its results of operations but there can be no assurance that such costs will not be material in the future or that such future compliance with existing, amended or new legal requirements will not have a material adverse effect on Florida Gas&amp;#8217; business and operating results.&amp;#160; Costs of planning, designing, constructing and operating pipelines and other facilities must incorporate compliance with environmental laws and regulations and safety standards.&amp;#160; Failure to comply with these laws and regulations may result in the assessment of administrative, civil and criminal penalties, the imposition of investigatory, remedial and corrective action obligations, natural resource damages, the issuance of injunctions in affected areas and the filing of federally authorized citizen suits.&amp;#160; Contingent losses related to all significant known environmental matters have been accrued and/or separately disclosed.&amp;#160; However, Florida Gas may revise accrual amounts prior to resolution of a particular contingency based on changes in facts and circumstances or changes in the expected outcome.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Environmental exposures and liabilities are difficult to assess and estimate due to unknown factors such as the magnitude of possible contamination, the timing and extent of remediation, the determination of Florida Gas&amp;#8217; liability in proportion to other parties, improvements in cleanup technologies and the extent to which environmental laws and regulations may change in the future.&amp;#160; Although environmental costs may have a significant impact on Florida Gas&amp;#8217; results of operations for any single period, Florida Gas believes that such costs will not have a material adverse effect on its financial position.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Air Quality Standards.&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;In 2023, the United States Environmental Protection Agency (&amp;#8220;EPA&amp;#8221;) finalized its Good Neighbor Plan (the &amp;#8220;Plan&amp;#8221;) which seeks to reduce nitrogen oxide pollution from power plants and other industrial facilities from 23 upwind states which the EPA determined is contributing to National Ambient Air Quality Standards (&amp;#8220;NAAQS&amp;#8221;) nonattainment and interfering with maintenance of the 2015 ozone NAAQS in downwind states. &amp;#160;As part of the Plan, the EPA announced that it would be issuing prescriptive emission standards for several sectors, including certain new and existing internal combustion engines of a certain size used in pipeline transportation of natural gas. &amp;#160;The EPA&amp;#8217;s final rule was to become effective on August 4, 2023, and the prescribed emission standards were scheduled to be effective in 2026. &amp;#160;However, on March 12, 2025, the EPA announced plans to end the Plan.&amp;#160; Operators and industry groups have challenged the Plan in the District of Columbia Circuit (&amp;#8220;D.C. Circuit&amp;#8221;), as well as the legal predicates to the individual upwind states&amp;#8217; inclusion in the Plan in the regional circuits.&amp;#160; The effectiveness of the rule is currently stayed in certain states, by nature of judicial stays of the legal predicate to the Plan, by judicial stay of the Plan itself by the United States Supreme Court (&amp;#8220;U.S. Supreme Court&amp;#8221;), or by the administrative stay issued by the EPA in October 2024.&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; mso-font-kerning: 1.0pt; mso-ligatures: standardcontextual;"&gt;On June 18, 2025, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that the regional circuits are the appropriate venue for the proceedings.&amp;#160; On July 30, 2025, the United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit placed the case in abeyance pending the EPA&amp;#8217;s reconsideration of its disapproval of upwind states&amp;#8217; state implementation plans addressing their Plan obligations.&amp;#160; Proceedings challenging the Plan in the D.C. Circuit were also placed in abeyance on May 2, 2025 pending the EPA&amp;#8217;s reconsideration of the Plan.&amp;#160; The EPA is preparing a proposed rulemaking as part of the reconsideration process and on January 27, 2026, the EPA announced its proposal to approve state implementation plans for certain states, which would resolve those states&amp;#8217; obligations under the Plan.&amp;#160; Florida Gas cannot predict with any certainty the substance of any later proposed rule or the potential impacts on Florida Gas.&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Florida Gas currently estimates that the existing final rule would require retrofitting or replacement of certain of its engines used in its operations.&amp;#160; Compliance with the Plan (if implementation is not stayed or otherwise delayed) may require substantial capital expenditures which could adversely affect Florida Gas&amp;#8217; business in future periods. &amp;#160;However, at this time, Florida Gas is still assessing the potential costs of this rule and, given uncertainties resulting from the multiple legal challenges filed against the Plan in various states, in the D.C. Circuit and the U.S. Supreme Court, Florida Gas cannot predict with any certainty what the final costs of compliance for the Plan for Florida Gas ultimately may be.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; text-align: justify; tab-stops: 0in; text-autospace: ideograph-numeric;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;"&gt;Other Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; tab-stops: list -9.0pt left 0in;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Federal Pipeline Integrity Rules.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160; Florida Gas&amp;#8217; pipeline operations are subject to regulation by the United States Department of Transportation under the Pipeline Hazardous Material Safety Administration (&amp;#8220;PHMSA&amp;#8221;), pursuant to which PHMSA has established requirements relating to the design, installation, testing, construction, operation, replacement and management of pipeline facilities.&amp;#160; Moreover, PHMSA, through the Office of Pipeline Safety, has promulgated a rule requiring pipeline operators to develop integrity management programs to comprehensively evaluate their pipelines, and take measures to protect pipeline segments located in what the rule refers to as &amp;#8220;high consequence areas&amp;#8221;.&amp;#160; Activities under these integrity management programs involve the performance of internal pipeline inspections, pressure testing or other effective means to assess the integrity of these regulated pipeline segments, and the regulations require prompt action to address integrity issues raised by the assessment and analysis.&amp;#160; Integrity testing and assessment of all of these assets will continue, and the results of such testing and assessment could cause Florida Gas to incur future capital and operating expenditures for repairs or upgrades deemed necessary to ensure the continued safe and reliable operation of Florida Gas.&amp;#160; Florida Gas incurred pipeline integrity costs of $9.7 million and $14.3 million for the three months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively.&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: .1in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Florida Gas&amp;#8217; operations are also subject to the requirements of the Federal Occupational Safety and Health Act (&amp;#8220;OSHA&amp;#8221;) and comparable state laws that regulate the protection of the health and safety of employees.&amp;#160; In addition, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration&amp;#8217;s hazardous communication standard requires that information be maintained about hazardous materials used or produced in Florida Gas&amp;#8217; operations and that this information be provided to employees, state and local government authorities and citizens.&amp;#160; Florida Gas believes that its past costs for OSHA required activities, including general industry standards, record keeping requirements and monitoring of occupational exposure to regulated substances have not had a material adverse effect on Florida Gas&amp;#8217; results of operations; however, there is no assurance that such costs will not be material in the future.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-left: -.1in; mso-hyphenate: none; tab-stops: -.5in 0in list 33.75pt;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; letter-spacing: -0.1pt;"&gt;NOTE C &amp;#8211; SUPPLEMENTAL CASH FLOW INFORMATION:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="line-height: 5.0pt; mso-line-height-rule: exactly;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
